  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 AM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nWe continue to monitor our next weather maker. Its a \nlow pressure system off the coast of southern California. It will \nmove inland today, then overhead on Friday, exiting the region on \nSaturday. There's been some noteworthy changes to the qpf forecast. \nNumbers have gone down across east Idaho and the Magic Valley while \nstaying about the same for the central mountains. The concern seems \nto be the amount of drier air over the region. As a result, we are \nseeing more convective elements than with the last system which was \nmore of a stratiform event. Expect clusters of showers and \nthunderstorms to move through central and east Idaho late Thursday \nafternoon, through Thursday night and into Friday morning. Most of \nthe deformation zone a.k.a. wrap around precipitation will stay \nacross the central mountains into the Island Park region on Friday. \nThe NBM mean qpf is now sitting just under an inch for east Idaho \nand the Magic Valley but still 1.00 to 1.50 inches across the \ncentral mountains into the Island Park region. The 50th percentile \nis just a tad under the mean. The 10th percentile is around 0.50 \ninches for east Idaho, 0.25 to 0.50 inches for the Magic Valley and \n0.50 to 1.00 inches for the central mountains into the Island Park \nregion. The 90th percentile is now 1.00 to 1.50 inches for east \nIdaho and the Magic Valley, and 1.50 to 2.00 inches for the central \nmountains. The official WPC forecast is on the dry end, now sitting \nbetween the 10th and 25th percentile. So, still a large range of \nvalues in the probabilities which indicates that there is still a \nlot of spread in the model solutions. However, the trend seems to be \nmoving drier. The snowfall forecast still hasn't changed much with \nmost of the snow falling above 7000 feet and mainly across the \ncentral mountains. Very little road impacts expected. Very noticeable \ndrop in temps expected from Thursday to Friday, roughly 20 degrees. \n\nDry weather is likely over the weekend into early next week. \nTemperatures will be a touch below average over the weekend and \nreturn to seasonable levels for next week.",
